add(1999,2,4,'Repeatedly splitting indistinguishable clubs gives a sharp n−1 separator bound; the singleton-club example proves optimality.','A set of people that distinguishes every club',r'A town has 99 nonempty clubs, no two with the same membership. Find the least number $n$ such that one can always choose at most $n$ people whose membership patterns distinguish every pair of clubs.',r'The answer is 98. Initially all clubs are in one class. If two clubs still have the same membership pattern on the chosen people, their distinct full membership lists provide a person who belongs to exactly one of them. Adding that person increases the number of pattern classes by at least one. After at most 98 additions all 99 clubs have different patterns. For sharpness, let the clubs be 99 different singleton sets. If at most 97 people are selected, two singleton clubs have no selected member and remain indistinguishable.','Combinatorics',['Sets','Algorithms','Separating systems','Extremal arguments'])
add(1999,3,5.5,'A common multiplier is constructed prime by prime using simultaneous congruences on valuations, with a distinct target exponent for each term.','Arbitrarily long progressions of perfect powers',r'For every integer $n>1$, prove that there are $n$ positive integers in a nonconstant arithmetic progression, each a perfect power with exponent greater than 1. Also give an example with three terms.',r'For three terms, $1,25,49$ are squares in arithmetic progression. For general $n$, choose distinct primes $q_1,\ldots,q_n$. For each prime $p\le n$, the Chinese remainder theorem supplies a nonnegative integer $e_p$ such that $e_p+v_p(i)\equiv0\pmod{q_i}$ for every $i=1,\ldots,n$. Put $M=\prod_{p\le n}p^{e_p}$. In the integer $Mi$, every prime exponent is divisible by $q_i$, so $Mi$ is a $q_i$th power. The terms $M,2M,\ldots,nM$ give the required progression.','Number Theory',['Chinese remainder theorem','Valuations','Perfect powers','Constructions'])
