add(1999,7,4,'A square congruent to 1 modulo a prime has a root congruent to ±1; expanding that root yields an explicit representation by p squares.','A sum of p squares from a square congruence',r'Let $p$ be prime and let $n$ be an integer such that $1+np$ is a perfect square. Prove that $n+1$ is a sum of $p$ integer squares.',r'Write $1+np=k^2$, taking $k\ge0$. Modulo $p$, $(k-1)(k+1)=0$, so $k=pt+\varepsilon$ for some integer $t$ and $\varepsilon\in\{1,-1\}$ (also when $p=2$). Expanding gives $n=pt^2+2\varepsilon t$. Hence $n+1=(t+\varepsilon)^2+(p-1)t^2$, an explicit sum of exactly $p$ squares.','Number Theory',['Congruences','Primes','Sum of squares'])
