add(2000,3,4.5,'Summation by parts pairs nonnegative partial sums with strictly decreasing reciprocal-product weights, also identifying every equality case.','Ordered products and reciprocal sums',r'Real numbers $x_i,y_i$ satisfy $0<x_1y_1<\cdots<x_ny_n$ and $\sum_{j=1}^i x_j\ge\sum_{j=1}^i y_j$ for every $i$. Prove $\sum 1/x_i\le\sum 1/y_i$ and determine equality.',r'Put $A_i=\sum_{j=1}^i(x_j-y_j)\ge0$ and $w_i=1/(x_iy_i)$, so $w_1>\cdots>w_n>0$. Summation by parts gives $\sum_{i=1}^n(1/y_i-1/x_i)=\sum_{i=1}^n(x_i-y_i)w_i=A_nw_n+\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}A_i(w_i-w_{i+1})\ge0$. Each coefficient is positive, so equality holds exactly when all $A_i=0$, equivalently $x_i=y_i$ for every $i$.','Algebra',['Inequalities','Summation by parts','Equality cases'])
add(2000,4,5.5,'Reflecting the construction in the triangle sides reduces it to the Steiner line of a circumcircle point; the reflected orthocentre supplies the key identification.','Four points on a Steiner line',r"Points $A,B,C,A',B',C'$ lie on a circle with $AA'\perp BC$, $BB'\perp CA$, $CC'\perp AB$. For another point $D$ on the circle, let $A''=DA'\cap BC$, $B''=DB'\cap CA$, and $C''=DC'\cap AB$. Prove that these three points and the orthocentre of $ABC$ are collinear.",r"Use complex coordinates on the unit circle, denoting vertices by lower-case letters. The orthocentre is $h=a+b+c$. Reflection in $BC$ sends a point $z$ to $b+c-bc\bar z$; thus it sends $H$ to $a'=-bc/a$, the second intersection of the altitude with the circle. Let $D_a,D_b,D_c$ be the reflections of $D$ in the three sides. Then $d_a-h=-(ad+bc)/d$, and cyclically $d_b-h=-(bd+ca)/d$, $d_c-h=-(cd+ab)/d$. Each of $ad+bc,bd+ca,cd+ab$ has conjugate equal to itself divided by $abcd$, so their ratios are real: $H,D_a,D_b,D_c$ are collinear. Reflecting the line $DA'$ in $BC$ gives $D_aH$; its intersection with the mirror line is unchanged. Hence $A''$ lies on that same line, and likewise $B'',C''$.",'Geometry',['Complex numbers','Reflections','Orthocentre','Collinearity','Steiner line'])
