add(2003,3,4.5,'Angle variables alone leave a parameter; the sine rule plus the isosceles condition then force a 30-degree relation.','An interior point that trisects an angle',r'In triangle $ABC$, $\angle ACB=2\angle ABC$. A point $D$ inside the triangle satisfies $AD=AC$ and $DB=DC$. Prove $\angle BAC=3\angle BAD$.',r'Write $\angle ABC=\beta$ and $\angle DBC=\angle BCD=t$. Then $\angle ACD=2\beta-t$, and $AD=AC$ gives $DC=2AC\cos(2\beta-t)$. Also $DB=DC$ gives $BC=2DC\cos t$. The sine rule in $ABC$ therefore yields $\sin3\beta/\sin\beta=4\cos t\cos(2\beta-t)$. Expanding both sides cancels $2\cos2\beta$ and leaves $2\cos(2\beta-2t)=1$. Because $0<\beta-t<60^\circ$, this gives $\beta-t=30^\circ$. Finally $\angle BAD=\beta-2t=60^\circ-\beta$, whereas $\angle BAC=180^\circ-3\beta$.','Geometry',['Sine rule','Trigonometry','Angle chasing','Isosceles triangles'],source=s)
add(2003,4,4.5,'Factoring p²−25 and pigeonholing the roots forces a product of many distinct nonzero integers to divide 10.','An integer-root bound for a squared polynomial',r'Let $p(x)$ be a monic integer polynomial of degree 2003. Prove that $p(x)^2-25$ has at most 2003 distinct integer roots.',r'If there were at least 2004, one of $p-5,p+5$ would have at least 1002 distinct integer roots $r_i$. Suppose it is $p-5$. Divide out their linear factors to get $p(x)-5=s(x)\prod_{i=1}^{1002}(x-r_i)$ with $s\in\mathbb Z[x]$. At any integer $m$ with $p(m)=-5$, the nonzero distinct integers $m-r_i$ would have product dividing $-10$. Their absolute product is at least $(501!)^2>10$, impossible. Thus every integer root belongs to $p-5$, which has degree 2003. The other sign is identical.','Algebra',['Integer polynomials','Factor theorem','Pigeonhole principle','Degree argument'],source=s)
