add(2006,2,4,'Multiplicativity turns strategically chosen comparisons 27<32 and 8<9 into bounds ruling out f(3)=7,8.','The least value of an increasing multiplicative function',r'A function $f:\mathbb N\to\mathbb N$ is strictly increasing and satisfies $f(ab)=f(a)f(b)$ and $f(3)\ge7$. What is the smallest possible $f(3)$?',r'The choice $f(n)=n^2$ attains 9. Since $f(1)=1$, we have $f(2)\ge2$. If $f(2)=2$, then $f(3)<f(4)=4$, impossible. If $f(2)=3$, monotonicity and multiplicativity give $f(27)=f(3)^3\ge343>243=f(32)$, again impossible. Thus $f(2)\ge4$, so $f(3)^2=f(9)>f(8)=f(2)^3\ge64$. As $f(3)$ is integral, it is at least 9.','Algebra',['Functional equations','Multiplicativity','Monotonicity'],source=s)
add(2006,3,None,'Trapezium with angle bisectors and parallel constructions read; complete trisection proof remains pending.',source=s)
add(2006,4,5.5,'The antipodal edge orbit determines the parity obstruction; the diameter claim then uses the relation between a graph and its complement.','A rotation that exchanges the two edge colours',r'Join every pair of $n$ labelled points on a circle. Colour each segment red or blue so that rotating both endpoints one position changes its colour. (a) For which $n$ can this be done? (b) Prove any two points can be connected using at most three red segments.',r'(a) Every edge orbit under rotation must have even length. Non-antipodal edges have orbit length $n$, and antipodal edges (when $n$ is even) have orbit length $n/2$. Hence $4\mid n$, which is also sufficient: alternate colours on each orbit. (For $n=1$ the edge-free case is vacuous.) (b) Rotation is an isomorphism between the red graph and its complement. If two vertices $x,y$ have no blue path of length at most two, then $xy$ is red and every other vertex has a red edge to at least one of $x,y$. Consequently every pair of vertices has a red path of length at most three, through $x,y$ if necessary. If no such pair $x,y$ exists, the blue graph has diameter at most two, and its isomorphic red graph does too.','Combinatorics',['Graph theory','Colouring','Graph complements','Parity'],source=s)
add(2006,5,3.5,'Coordinates in a unit square give the two circumcentres explicitly; their vectors are perpendicular and equal in length, with sum E.',source=s)
add(2006,6,2.5,'The digit product is bounded by n; the resulting quadratic bounds leave only n=4,13,14 to inspect.',source=s)
add(2006,7,4.5,'Stabilisation propagates from the last coordinate to the first; once the tail is fixed, a coordinate iterates a bounded nondecreasing integer map.','Repeated inversion-count offspring',r'For a finite sequence of nonnegative integers, replace each term by the number of smaller terms to its right. Repeat this operation. Prove that eventually the sequence stops changing.',r'The last entry becomes 0 after one operation and stays so. Work backward by induction. Suppose all entries to the right of position $i$ have stabilised at $c_{i+1},\ldots,c_n$. Thereafter position $i$ iterates the map $h(t)=\#\{j>i:c_j<t\}$. This map is nondecreasing and takes values in $\{0,\ldots,n-i\}$. If an iterate is at least its predecessor, monotonicity makes all later iterates nondecreasing; if it is at most its predecessor, all later iterates are nonincreasing. A bounded monotone integer sequence stabilises. Thus position $i$ eventually stabilises too. Induction reaches the first entry.','Combinatorics',['Monovariants','Sequences','Algorithms','Induction'],source=s)
add(2006,8,4.5,'Any two candidates can be separated by a prime/composite translate; the separating query comes from the first composite term in a finite prime-started arithmetic progression.','Guessing an integer using prime sums',r'Alice chooses an integer $a$ with $1\le a\le2006$. On each turn Bob names a positive integer $b$ and learns whether $a+b$ is prime. Prove he can always determine $a$ in fewer than 2006 turns.',r'It suffices to separate any two remaining candidates $u<v$. Put $d=v-u$ and choose a prime $q>v$. The progression $q,q+d,q+2d,\ldots$ starts with a prime, but its term $q+qd=q(1+d)$ is composite. Let $k\ge1$ be the first index with $q+kd$ composite. Then $b=q+(k-1)d-u>0$ makes $u+b$ prime and $v+b$ composite. This query eliminates at least one of the candidates. Repeat while more than one remains. Starting from 2006 candidates requires at most 2005 queries.','Number Theory',['Primes','Arithmetic progressions','Algorithms','Logic'],source=s)
